{"caseId":"OD2-HO-STOP-2","answers":[{"itemId":"I-H1","answer":"contradicted","confidence":85,"evidenceIds":["E3","E2","E1"]},{"itemId":"I-H2","answer":"contradicted","confidence":82,"evidenceIds":["E2","E3"]},{"itemId":"I-H3","answer":"supported","confidence":75,"evidenceIds":["E1","E3","E2"]},{"itemId":"I-H4","answer":"unknown","confidence":75,"evidenceIds":["E6","E2"]},{"itemId":"I-H5","answer":"contradicted","confidence":88,"evidenceIds":["E2","E6"]},{"itemId":"I-R1","answer":"contradicted","confidence":75,"evidenceIds":["E2","E3"]},{"itemId":"I-R2","answer":"causal","confidence":92,"evidenceIds":["E1"]},{"itemId":"I-R3","answer":"contradicted","confidence":72,"evidenceIds":["E5","E3"]},{"itemId":"I-R4","answer":"association-only","confidence":65,"evidenceIds":["E5"]},{"itemId":"I-A1","answer":"preferred","confidence":72,"evidenceIds":["E1","E2","E3"]},{"itemId":"I-A2","answer":"harmful","confidence":75,"evidenceIds":["E3","E2"]},{"itemId":"I-A3","answer":"harmful","confidence":93,"evidenceIds":["E2","E4"]},{"itemId":"I-A4","answer":"premature","confidence":80,"evidenceIds":["E6"]},{"itemId":"I-A5","answer":"acceptable","confidence":70,"evidenceIds":["E6","E2"]},{"itemId":"I-SUFF","answer":"sufficient","confidence":75,"evidenceIds":["E1","E2","E3","E5","E6"]}],"brief":"This looks like one chance QC flag, not a faulty analyzer or a bad reagent lot. The failed Level 2 result passed on repeat. The other two levels and sodium and chloride were fine. Level 2 averages are unchanged since the lot change. 43 patient samples re-run on Analyzer A agreed with Analyzer B within 0.10 mmol/L. The patient median is steady. All 182 scheduled QC runs were done on time, so the short-staffing theory isn't supported. About one chance flag a month is expected at this QC volume. I can't say whether a sampling glitch or plain chance caused it, so don't record a cause as confirmed.

Ward 6's high potassiums are split evenly between Analyzer A and Analyzer B. They coincide with the tube outage, which delays samples, and with four renal overflow patients. Both are plausible explanations, but neither is proven.

What to sign off: return Analyzer B to potassium service and close the review of the 186 results with no amendments. Optionally add extra Level 2 QC for a week. Don't widen the limits. Don't recall about 2,900 results or quarantine the lot. Ask Ward 6 to send repeat samples, and get the tube repaired."}
